>

  <p class="lede">The training repo grew faster than any one Claude could keep in head. Generic Claude could read all of it. Generic Claude could not safely <em>edit</em> all of it without lane-crossing into territory it shouldn't touch — fixing a Python bug by also "improving" the bat that invoked it by also tweaking the mod that emitted the schema the bug was about. So I started carving the work into specialists.</p>

  <p>Nine specialists in <code>.claude/agents/&lt;name&gt;.md</code>. They cluster into five <strong>lanes</strong> — planning, code, ops, advisory, comms — and the v5 treatment leans on that taxonomy explicitly. Spec stanzas, marginalia pills, and the floated rule-cards all carry the lane color.</p>

  <p>By the time the project was three weeks old it was 100+ files spanning a Python PPO learner, a C# Harmony mod, a fleet of bat scripts driving 11 Windows laptops over SSH, and a tangle of dashboard and eval and run-state machinery on top. Nine specialists got carved out in about a day and a half in late April. Each one lives at <code>.claude/agents/&lt;name&gt;.md</code> in the <code>sts2Player</code> repo. Each one has a frontmatter block declaring <code>name</code>, <code>description</code>, <code>tools</code>, and <code>model</code>, then a long prose spec defining what it owns, what it explicitly cedes, the artifacts it emits, and the hard rules whose violation stops the engagement. Nine specialists plus one top-level orchestrator (Claude itself, in the conversation), with the user — me — gating every meaningful decision.</p>

  <p>This is a tour of the crew. Who they are, why each one got carved out, what the hard rules are, and the war stories that sharpened them. The patterns transfer; the specifics are mine.</p>

  <h2 id="what-is"><span class="num">§ 1</span>What a subagent actually is, briefly</h2>
  <svg class="under" viewBox="0 0 90 8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M 2 4 Q 18 1, 32 5 T 60 3 T 86 5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
  <p>For readers new to Claude Code's subagent feature: a subagent is a separately-instantiated Claude with its own system prompt, its own tool whitelist, and its own scoped context window. The orchestrator (the active Claude Code session) summons a subagent with a task; the subagent executes in its own context with only the files it pulls in; it returns artifacts and a summary; the orchestrator integrates. The point isn't capacity — it's <em>isolation</em>. The subagent can't accidentally edit a file outside its declared scope because its system prompt told it not to and its task description didn't ask it to. With a careful spec, the subagent's boundary is the spec.</p>

  <p>That isolation is what makes a multi-agent stack viable in a 100+-file repo. One conversation can't hold all of it; nine conversations each holding a slice can.</p>

