  <h3 id="bb"><span class="ham">#</span>blueprint-beacon · the planner</h3>
  <div class="spec" data-lane="planning">
    <div class="spec-bar"><span class="file">.claude/agents/blueprint-beacon.md</span><span class="right"><span class="lane-tag">planning</span><span class="model opus">opus</span></span></div>
    <div class="spec-body"><table>
      <tr><th>nickname</th><td><code>bb</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th>lane</th><td>planning</td></tr>
      <tr><th>added</th><td>2026-04-22 · first wave</td></tr>
      <tr><th>owns</th><td><code>drafts/bb/*</code> · BB-NNN ledger · plan authorship · cross-agent routing</td></tr>
      <tr><th>cedes</th><td>implementation (pp), C# (mm), fleet (ww), measurement (ee), advisory (nn)</td></tr>
      <tr><th>triggers</th><td>manual</td></tr>
      <tr><th>signature</th><td>long-route critique cycle (9 phases)</td></tr>
    </table></div>
  </div>
  <div class="rule-float" data-lane="planning">
    <div class="rf-bar"><span>hard rule · bb</span><span>load-bearing</span></div>
    <div class="rf-body">no work happens without user approval. no exceptions. <em>approval of phase 1 does NOT roll forward to phase 2.</em></div>
  </div>
  <p><code>bb</code> was the first specialist. The reason was simple: I kept asking generic Claude "what should we work on next?" and getting answers that were either "improve training" (too vague) or "change <code>entropy_coef</code> to 0.005" (too narrow, no surrounding context). I wanted both layers — the <em>immediate</em> next task and the <em>milestone</em> it serves — and I wanted approval gates between every step.</p>

  <p><code>bb</code> is the only agent on <code>opus</code>. Every other agent runs <code>sonnet</code>. Planning gets the bigger model.</p>

  <p>The plans are layered. Every BB-NNN proposal has to address three horizons at once:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>The active 1–3 month milestone the work serves.</li>
    <li>The phased breakdown of how to get there.</li>
    <li>The immediate next task — concrete enough to spawn an agent against.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Missing any layer means the plan is incomplete and gets rejected at draft. This rule started as a war story. Early <code>bb</code> plans tried to defer concrete decisions ("phase 7 will figure that out"), and "phase 7" never showed up. The hard rule was the fix: every plan layers all three horizons, every time.</p>

  <h4>signature · long-route critique cycle</h4>
  <pre class="field-block"><div class="pre-tag"><span>drafts/bb/bb-plans.md  ·  long-route ladder (9 phases)</span></div><code>{`bb-initial-draft
    ↓
critique-round-1   (parallel pp · mm · ee · nn — only the lanes the proposal touches)
    ↓
bb-revise-1        (folds critiques with FOLDED / DEFERRED / REJECTED disposition)
    ↓
critique-round-2   (rotating emphasis — failure modes, perf, etc.)
    ↓
bb-revise-2
    ↓
critique-round-3
    ↓
bb-final
    ↓
claude cold review (orchestrator end-to-end; flags drift; small targeted fix)
    ↓
OneDrive copy → user gate`}</code></pre>

  <p>Three rotating critique cycles. Each cycle produces a critique doc per agent, folded into the plan with explicit disposition (FOLDED / DEFERRED / REJECTED) tracked in a matrix at the top of the plan. There's a FAST route too — four phases, one critique cycle — for single-file work that doesn't merit the full nine-phase grinder.</p>

  <p>The <code>bb</code> ledger lives at <code>drafts/bb/bb-plans.md</code> and assigns monotonic <code>BB-NNN</code> ids. Every other agent's reports cite the BB-NNN they trace back to. It's the spine of the project's memory.</p>

