  <h3 id="pp"><span class="ham">#</span>python-phantom · the residual editor</h3>
  <div class="spec" data-lane="code">
    <div class="spec-bar"><span class="file">.claude/agents/python-phantom.md</span><span class="right"><span class="lane-tag">code</span><span class="model sonnet">sonnet</span></span></div>
    <div class="spec-body"><table>
      <tr><th>nickname</th><td><code>pp</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th>lane</th><td>code</td></tr>
      <tr><th>added</th><td>2026-04-22 · first wave</td></tr>
      <tr><th>owns</th><td><code>src/bot/*.py</code> · <code>src/bot/run_state_*.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th>cedes</th><td>C# + <code>launch_loop.bat</code> → mm; planning → bb; fleet bats → ww</td></tr>
      <tr><th>triggers</th><td>manual + reactive to bb (on user-approved phase)</td></tr>
      <tr><th>signature</th><td>3-tier verification ladder · min / med / heavy</td></tr>
    </table></div>
  </div>
  <div class="rule-float" data-lane="code">
    <div class="rf-bar"><span>hard rule · pp</span><span>verification</span></div>
    <div class="rf-body">every diff carries a verification block. <em>warm-start success messages reflect actual copy, not "we got past the function."</em></div>
  </div>
  <p>After <code>bb</code>, <code>pp</code>. The reason was the lane-crossing problem. Generic Claude would fix a Python bug by also editing a bat file by also tweaking the mod, and I'd have to read three lanes of diff to understand a single change. <code>pp</code> solves this with a "residual + adaptive" framing — <code>pp</code> owns whatever isn't explicitly someone else's, and as new specialists arrive, <code>pp</code> politely cedes territory.</p>

  <p>Today <code>pp</code> owns:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>src/bot/*.py</code> — the whole Python side. PPO learner, model, rewards, stages, env, protocol, encoders, the training-side <code>actor_proxy</code>, episode logger, dashboard backend, plot, stub.</li>
    <li><code>src/bot/run_state_*.py</code> — the suite of pretrain/eval/data/model/config/live modules.</li>
    <li><code>worker_config.toml.example</code> (when a shape change is Python-driven; the per-machine <code>worker_config.toml</code> is gitignored and stays in user territory).</li>
    <li>One-off utility scripts that aren't fleet orchestration.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><code>pp</code> cedes: C# mod and <code>launch_loop.bat</code> to <code>mm</code>, planning to <code>bb</code>, fleet bats to <code>ww</code>, the <code>sts2Stats</code> sibling repo to nobody (read-only).</p>

  <p>The signature feature is a three-tier verification ladder. Every <code>pp</code> edit comes back with the verification result baked into the diff summary:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Minimum:</strong> <code>python -m py_compile &lt;files&gt;</code> — always.</li>
    <li><strong>Medium:</strong> import sanity (<code>python -c "import src.bot.&lt;module&gt;"</code>) for cross-module or public-API edits.</li>
    <li><strong>Heavy:</strong> an actual smoke run for hot-path edits — model, PPO loss, checkpoint loading, protocol. The smoke script gets authored under <code>drafts/pp/current/&lt;date&gt;-&lt;slug&gt;/</code> if no existing one fits.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>signature · verification baked into every diff</h4>
  <pre class="field-block"><div class="pre-tag"><span>drafts/pp/current/2026-05-02-shop-warmstart/test.py · output</span></div><code>{`[verification] tier: heavy
[verification] python -m py_compile src/bot/run_state_shop_live.py: PASS
[verification] python -c "import src.bot.run_state_shop_live": PASS
[verification] smoke: drafts/pp/current/2026-05-02-shop-warmstart/test.py: PASS
  - load picker ckpt_147900.pt -> ShopModel
  - 4 embeddings copied (card/enemy/relic/power)
  - all 4 frozen (requires_grad=False)
  - bit-for-bit identical to picker source`}</code></pre>

  <p>That last line is doing a lot of work. The bug it covers — the "warm-start complete" lie — is the kind of thing this whole crew structure is built to catch. The ShopModel learner was logging <code>Warm-start complete. card=521 enemy=69 relic=128 power=64 frozen.</code> while <em>also</em> logging four <code>WARNING: card_emb.weight not in picker checkpoint — using random init</code> lines for those exact tables. The "complete" message fired even when nothing was actually copied. The picker checkpoint nests its state_dict under a top-level <code>'policy'</code> key; the loader was looking at the top level and missing every embedding. <code>pp</code>'s fix was a 12-line defensive unwrap. The verification proved the embeddings were now bit-for-bit identical to the picker source, frozen, and matched.</p>

  <p>The lesson got promoted: warm-start success messages should reflect actual copy, not "we got past the function."</p>

  <h3 id="mm"><span class="ham">#</span>mod-man · C# / Harmony</h3>
  <div class="spec" data-lane="code">
    <div class="spec-bar"><span class="file">.claude/agents/mod-man.md</span><span class="right"><span class="lane-tag">code</span><span class="model sonnet">sonnet</span></span></div>
    <div class="spec-body"><table>
      <tr><th>nickname</th><td><code>mm</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th>lane</th><td>code</td></tr>
      <tr><th>added</th><td>2026-04-22 · first wave</td></tr>
      <tr><th>owns</th><td><code>src/AutoSlayRestartMod/</code> · <code>scripts/worker/launch_loop.bat</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th>cedes</th><td>Python sidecars → pp; fleet bats → ww; planning → bb</td></tr>
      <tr><th>triggers</th><td>manual</td></tr>
      <tr><th>signature</th><td>5 hard rules (bat <code>.bak</code>, schema flag, save-compat, game-update stop, auto-rollback)</td></tr>
    </table></div>
  </div>
  <div class="rule-float" data-lane="code">
    <div class="rf-bar"><span>hard rule · mm</span><span>game-update stop</span></div>
    <div class="rf-body">if Steam ships an update and <code>Sts2Patcher.exe</code> fails or a <code>[HarmonyPatch]</code> target type doesn't resolve — <em>stop.</em> no improvising.</div>
  </div>
  <p><code>mm</code> came in with the first wave because the C# mod work is foreign territory for general Claude. Harmony attribute syntax, Mono.Cecil internals, the <code>[ModInitializer]</code> lifecycle, the patcher's IL emissions — all of it needs specialist context. <code>mm</code>'s spec embeds primary-surface reference (per-file documentation of every <code>[HarmonyPatch]</code> class) so the agent has anchored context every invocation, not just when the conversation happens to have read those files recently.</p>

  <p><code>mm</code> owns:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><code>src/AutoSlayRestartMod/</code> — the whole Harmony mod, every <code>[HarmonyPatch]</code> class, the canonical mod↔proxy JSON schema.</li>
    <li><code>scripts/worker/launch_loop.bat</code> — the worker-side build+inject loop that does <code>dotnet build</code> → xcopy DLL into the game's mods dir → run <code>Sts2Patcher.exe</code> against <code>sts2.dll</code> → infinite-loop <code>SlayTheSpire2.exe --autoslay --seed &lt;hex&gt;</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>The hard rules stack up because the C# side has the most ways to silently break the rest of the stack:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Bat backup.</strong> Any edit to <code>launch_loop.bat</code> gets a <code>.bak</code> copy alongside <em>before</em> the first edit lands.</li>
    <li><strong>Schema changes called out.</strong> Any edit changing the JSON shape the mod sends to <code>actor_proxy</code> must explicitly flag training-side paired work. Bump <code>RunStateSerializer.RunStateSchemaVersion</code> in the same change.</li>
    <li><strong>Save-compat.</strong> Existing PyTorch checkpoints must still load through the partial-load fallback. <code>data/stage.json</code> must remain backward-readable by older mod builds.</li>
    <li><strong>Game-update stop.</strong> If Steam ships an update and <code>Sts2Patcher.exe</code> fails or a <code>[HarmonyPatch]</code> target type doesn't resolve — <strong>stop</strong>. No improvising. Write up what broke and hand back.</li>
    <li><strong>Auto-rollback.</strong> If I report a mod-side regression traced to <code>mm</code>'s edit, <code>mm</code> restores from <code>.bak</code> (bats) or <code>git checkout -- &lt;file&gt;</code> (C#) automatically and logs the rollback under the same MM-NNN with a <code>Rolled back</code> line.</li>
  </ol>

  <h4>war story · the 5-iteration shop wire-up (BB-035)</h4>
  <table class="iter-table">
    <thead><tr><th>id</th><th>change</th><th>status</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>MM-067</td><td><code>Task.Run</code> fire-and-forget</td><td class="fail">✗ raced w/ teardown</td></tr>
      <tr><td>MM-068</td><td>sync <code>.GetAwaiter().GetResult()</code></td><td class="fail">✗ still stalled</td></tr>
      <tr><td>MM-069</td><td>raw <code>NetworkStream</code> + <code>FlushAsync</code> + <code>ConfigureAwait(false)</code></td><td class="ok">✓ flushed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>MM-070</td><td>file-based <code>data/eor_diag.log</code> diagnostic</td><td class="ok">✓ proved call</td></tr>
      <tr><td>MM-071</td><td><em>(in pp)</em> add <code>schema_version</code> to fanout payload</td><td class="ok">✓ ROOT</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The signature war story is the 5-iteration shop wire-up during BB-035. The mod's <code>Phase3Bridge.SendEndOfRun</code> had to also notify <code>picker_sidecar:9996</code> with an end-of-run envelope so the shop_sidecar fanout chain triggered. Five iterations:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>MM-067</strong> — <code>Task.Run</code> fire-and-forget. Never executed in production (raced with game-over teardown).</li>
    <li><strong>MM-068</strong> — sync <code>.GetAwaiter().GetResult()</code>. Still didn't reach <code>picker_sidecar</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>MM-069</strong> — raw <code>NetworkStream.WriteAsync</code> + <code>FlushAsync</code> + drain read, replacing <code>StreamWriter</code> (which was sending TCP FIN before bytes flushed). Codex caught a HOLD finding pre-land — <code>ConfigureAwait(false)</code> needed.</li>
    <li><strong>MM-070</strong> — file-based diagnostic logging to <code>data/eor_diag.log</code>. Proved the method WAS being called and bytes WERE being sent.</li>
    <li><strong>MM-071</strong> — root cause finally identified, in <code>picker_sidecar.py</code> (so <code>pp</code>'s lane via dispatch, not <code>mm</code>'s): the fanout payload to <code>shop_sidecar</code> was missing <code>schema_version</code>, and <code>shop_sidecar</code> was rejecting it silently. 1-line fix landed downstream.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><em>Five iterations on the C# side, none of which were the actual bug.</em> The bug was a missing field in a Python sidecar's outgoing payload. The reason it took five iterations is that <code>mm</code>'s contract is to fix what's in <code>mm</code>'s lane and report; without the diagnostic logging in MM-070, nobody on the team would have known to look downstream. Each iteration went through codex review before land per the standing <code>feedback_codex_review_loop</code> rule. The pattern — keep adding observability until the failure surface narrows enough to point at the right lane — is what the multi-agent setup is for. A monolithic Claude editing freely across all three lanes would have made the shop work in one round and never written down why.</p>

