  <h3 id="ww"><span class="ham">#</span>worker-warden · fleet guardian</h3>
  <div class="spec" data-lane="ops">
    <div class="spec-bar"><span class="file">.claude/agents/worker-warden.md</span><span class="right"><span class="lane-tag">ops</span><span class="model sonnet">sonnet</span></span></div>
    <div class="spec-body"><table>
      <tr><th>nickname</th><td><code>ww</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th>lane</th><td>ops</td></tr>
      <tr><th>added</th><td>2026-04-22 · first wave</td></tr>
      <tr><th>owns</th><td>worker health · TCP session health · fleet throughput · deployment pipeline · SSH orchestration</td></tr>
      <tr><th>cedes</th><td>code-side bugs to pp/mm (ww only restarts; never edits)</td></tr>
      <tr><th>triggers</th><td>manual + scheduled (<code>/loop</code>) + reactive (event-signature match)</td></tr>
      <tr><th>signature</th><td>auto-recovery · single-worker soft only · <strong>no <code>git pull</code></strong></td></tr>
    </table></div>
  </div>
  <div class="rule-float" data-lane="ops">
    <div class="rf-bar"><span>hard rule · ww</span><span>auto-recovery scope</span></div>
    <div class="rf-body">single-worker soft failures only. multiple workers down → STOP. learner-up-but-no-workers → STOP. fleet-wide throughput collapse → STOP. <em>no <code>git pull</code> on auto-recovery.</em></div>
  </div>
  <p><code>ww</code> came in when fleet-debugging conversations with generic Claude were eating hours. Every worker outage was being triaged from scratch, with no shared context about what soft-vs-hard signatures looked like, no rolling log of past incidents, and no scoped auto-restart authority. <code>ww</code>'s spec is the most operationally complex of the crew because it's the one with the closest thing to autonomous action.</p>

  <p><code>ww</code> owns the fleet:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Worker health — alive/dead, throughput, last-heartbeat age, per-worker process tree.</li>
    <li>TCP session health — <code>actor_proxy ↔ learner</code> pickle-framed socket, the game-side <code>RlBridge</code> newline-JSON bridge, stale sockets, infinite reconnect loops.</li>
    <li>Training throughput across the fleet.</li>
    <li>The deployment pipeline — <code>scripts/main/deploy.bat</code> → <code>scripts/worker/run_worker.bat</code> → <code>scripts/worker/launch_loop.bat</code>.</li>
    <li>SSH orchestration — <code>restart_workers.bat</code>, <code>deploy.bat</code>, <code>kill_all_terminals.bat</code>, <code>setup_ssh.bat</code>, <code>full_relaunch.bat</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><code>ww</code> is the only agent with three trigger modes:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Manual</strong> — I summon it.</li>
    <li><strong>Scheduled</strong> — periodic fleet sweeps via my <code>/loop</code> skill or <code>CronCreate</code>. Quiet unless something's off.</li>
    <li><strong>Reactive</strong> — fires when an event signature matches: worker dark &gt; N min, TCP drop, throughput collapse, checkpoints not advancing.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>It's the only agent with auto-recovery authority, and the spec is paranoid about scoping that authority. <strong>Single-worker soft failures only.</strong> <code>ww</code> may auto-restart one worker without asking on these signatures:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>actor_proxy</code> silently stops pushing while peers are still pushing (<code>workers.json</code> shows <code>last_seen_s</code> past the learner's refresh cadence for that one worker).</li>
    <li><code>actor_proxy ↔ learner</code> TCP drop with the proxy's manual reconnect failing.</li>
    <li>Game process crashes mid-episode and <code>launch_loop.bat</code>'s 5s-timeout restart loop hasn't recovered.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Auto-recovery is single-worker only. No <code>git pull</code> (preserves code state — anti-diagnostic to change code while debugging). Every auto-recovery fires a proactive notification:</p>

  <h4>auto-recovery emit</h4>
  <pre class="field-block"><div class="pre-tag"><span>ntfy.sh push · ww auto-recovery notification</span></div><code>{`ww auto-recovery: stsBot7 actor_proxy stalled (last_seen_s=620, peers fresh).
Restarted via SSH (no git pull). Recovery confirmed within 30s of restart.
Logged at drafts/ww/fleet-log.md.`}</code></pre>

  <p>The hard-error set — stop and flag, no auto-recovery — is everything systemic:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Multiple workers down at once.</li>
    <li>Learner reachable but no worker reporting (likely bad config or port conflict or wire-format mismatch).</li>
    <li>Sudden fleet-wide throughput collapse with no individual worker signature.</li>
    <li>Checksum mismatches on deployed binaries — that smell is a Steam game update, which fires <code>mm</code>'s game-update-stop rule.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A few war stories that sharpened the rules:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The <code>/dev/null</code> regression in <code>deploy.bat</code>.</strong> Bash-style redirections (<code>&gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1</code>) had crept into a Windows .bat file. cmd.exe interpreted <code>/dev/null</code> as a literal path <code>\dev\null</code> and failed with "The system cannot find the path specified." right after the first <code>[stsBot0] Killing processes...</code> echo. <code>ww</code> root-caused 13 occurrences, fixed all to <code>&gt;nul 2&gt;&amp;1</code>, audited the other 6 fleet bats (clean). The kind of regression that's three minutes to write into existence and an hour to track down without a fleet specialist.</li>
    <li><strong>The UNC admin-share trap.</strong> <code>deploy.bat</code> was using <code>\\host\C$\repos\sts2Player</code> (UNC admin share) for file distribution. That requires Administrator credentials on every worker, which I didn't have configured. <code>ww</code> rewrote Phase 2 to use <code>scp</code> over SSH (same auth path as the working SSH-kill / SSH-schtasks phases). Thirty minutes of work that unblocked deployment.</li>
    <li><strong>The double-<code>ww</code> auto-restart conflict.</strong> Two parallel <code>ww</code> scans both had auto-restart authority on the same worker. The second restart fired during the first restart's <code>taskkill</code> phase, killing the box. The memory file <code>feedback_parallel_ww_dispatch_conflict</code> codified the rule: concurrent <code>ww</code> scans must not BOTH have restart authority on the same worker.</li>
    <li><strong>The pinned-picker-checkpoint race.</strong> <code>restart_workers.bat</code> was scp'ing "latest picker ckpt by mtime" but the picker writes new checkpoints every few minutes. Between me warm-starting ShopModel against <code>ckpt_148210.pt</code> and running <code>restart_workers.bat</code>, the latest had drifted to <code>ckpt_148245.pt</code>. Workers got a <code>picker_checkpoint.pt</code> whose hash didn't match the <code>shop_warmstart_meta.json</code> they also got. Hash gate failed. <code>ww</code> rewrote the detection to read the path from <code>shop_warmstart_meta.json</code> (pinned source) with a latest-by-mtime fallback for cold-start. Codex caught a HOLD finding on the second pass — a silent <code>catch {}</code> would have recreated the bug.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><code>ww</code> also has a strict PII rule: real worker LAN IPs and hostnames are personal. Every user-visible artifact uses <code>stsBot[N]</code> placeholders. The rule is in every agent's spec but <code>ww</code> is the one that emits a fleet log every day, so it's the agent where the rule gets exercised most.</p>

