  <p><code>gg</code> was added in the first wave for a specific failure mode: agents were independently re-deriving the same game mechanics and sometimes getting them wrong. Four agents independently looking up the Bash damage formula and getting different answers was a real thing that happened.</p>

  <p><code>gg</code> is pure reference. He answers questions about how <em>Slay the Spire 2</em> actually behaves — card effects, damage formulas, intent timing, status/power mechanics, save-file format, what game-state fields reflection exposes, beta-build patch differences, strategy and meta info. He doesn't edit code. He doesn't pick patch strategy. He doesn't plan.</p>

  <p>Source priority is a hard rule:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><code>sts2Stats/</code> — sibling repo with extracted decompiled assets.</li>
    <li>Live <code>sts2.dll</code> decompile via <code>ilspycmd</code>.</li>
    <li>STS2 official patch notes.</li>
    <li>Community wikis.</li>
    <li>In-repo design docs.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Game files always beat web info on conflict. And every external claim gets flagged as beta-applicable or <code>⚠ version unknown</code> because I'm on STS2 BETA, not release, and the two diverge constantly.</p>

  <p>The signature output is a Drift Flag — concrete file/line pointers when current code diverges from game reality. The classic example: during BB-019 boss-win capture work, <code>gg</code> flagged that <code>KIN_FOLLOWER</code> (encounter id) ≠ <code>THE_KIN_BOSS</code> (boss id). The mod's <code>boss_id</code> capture was reading the wrong field. Drift flag pointed at <code>Phase3BossVictoryPatch.cs</code>. <code>mm</code> picked it up via the orchestrator on the next engagement.</p>

  <p><code>gg</code> doesn't auto-fix anything. He flags. The flag sits in the report and the relevant agent picks it up. This decouples discovery from action and prevents two agents from independently fixing the same thing in different ways.</p>

  <p>There's a deliberate departure from the rest of the crew here: <code>gg</code> has no monotonic id ledger. The other report-emitting agents have BB-NNN, EE-NNN, MM-NNN, DD-NNN patterns. <code>gg</code>'s reports are dated but unnumbered, because game facts get re-derived per question — there's no value in cross-citing "as gg established in GG-014" when the next question gets a fresh answer from the same source files anyway.</p>

  <h3 id="nn"><span class="ham">#</span>neural-nomad · ML architect (advisory)</h3>
  <div class="spec" data-lane="advisory">
    <div class="spec-bar"><span class="file">.claude/agents/neural-nomad.md</span><span class="right"><span class="lane-tag">advisory</span><span class="model sonnet">sonnet</span></span></div>
    <div class="spec-body"><table>
      <tr><th>nickname</th><td><code>nn</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th>lane</th><td>advisory</td></tr>
      <tr><th>added</th><td>2026-04-22/23 · second wave</td></tr>
      <tr><th>owns</th><td>pure advisory. proposes; pp implements after user-approved phase</td></tr>
      <tr><th>cedes</th><td>all code edits. forever.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>triggers</th><td>manual + reactive to bb</td></tr>
      <tr><th>signature</th><td>4 hot-path rules (input shape, entropy/LR/clip, new heads, reward scale)</td></tr>
    </table></div>
  </div>
  <div class="rule-float" data-lane="advisory">
    <div class="rf-bar"><span>hard rule · nn</span><span>new-head PPO interaction</span></div>
    <div class="rf-body">never propose adding a new head without naming PPO-loss interaction. <em>"ambiguous" is not an acceptable answer.</em></div>
  </div>
  <p><code>nn</code> came in shortly after <code>pp</code>. The reason was that <code>pp</code> was making subtle ML-design choices inside implementation tasks — picking head shapes, choosing init schemes, picking entropy coefs — without surfacing the design space or the trade-offs. The choices were defensible but not auditable. <code>nn</code>'s lane separates design from implementation; the design space gets explicit treatment + user approval before implementation lands.</p>

  <p><code>nn</code> is pure advisory. He never edits code. He proposes; <code>pp</code> implements (after the user-approved phase).</p>

  <p>The hard rules are all hot-path. Violating them silently corrupts training:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Never propose an input-vector shape change without addressing checkpoint compatibility explicitly.</strong> Includes dimensional delta, compat statement, warm-start plan or transfer plan, and a small-scale verification probe.</li>
    <li><strong>Never bump entropy coef / LR / clip ratio without a 1-run ablation plan.</strong> Compare policy entropy + return curves + loss curves under old and new values.</li>
    <li><strong>Never propose adding a new head without naming PPO-loss interaction.</strong> Is it on-policy? Does its log_prob mix into the importance-sampling ratio? If yes, can the learner re-evaluate it during PPO update? "Ambiguous" is not an acceptable answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Never change reward scale without auditing downstream value-function drift.</strong> Includes current V range (empirical), expected post-change range, and val-loss monitoring plan.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Rule 3 has a name on it. The cautionary tale is the <code>choice_proj</code> silent-corruption episode — a new head was added to the policy without explicit treatment of how its log_prob interacted with PPO's importance-sampling ratio. The head produced decisions that contributed to the rollout actor's log_prob stream, but the learner couldn't re-evaluate them during PPO update. Result: the importance-ratio was silently corrupted, training drifted invisibly for hours before anyone noticed. Rule 3 was carved out the next day.</p>

  <h4>proposal shape · BB-035 cycle excerpt</h4>
  <pre class="field-block"><div class="pre-tag"><span>drafts/nn/proposals/2026-04-26-shop-encoder.md</span></div><code>{`Encoder option A — 226 dims, slot-concatenated:
  pros: matches picker's input layout convention; easy to reason about
        per-slot.
  cons: brittle to permutation of card slots in shop offerings.

Encoder option B — 225 dims, mean-pooled per category:
  pros: permutation-invariant; smaller dim → fewer params → faster forward.
  cons: loses per-slot structure (can't condition on "the third card slot
        specifically").

Recommendation: B. Reasoning: (a) shop offerings are a SET, not a sequence,
so permutation invariance is structurally correct; (b) the policy head's
10-action space already encodes the slot positions, so the encoder doesn't
need to.

⚠ This recommendation leans on Vinyals et al. 2016 (Order Matters: Sequence
to Sequence for Sets) — set-encoder literature establishes mean-pooling
as the standard permutation-invariant choice when set order is semantically
meaningless. The user should weigh this if the slot order in shop offerings
turns out to carry signal we haven't anticipated.`}</code></pre>

  <p>The <code>⚠</code> is a hard-rule output: any recommendation sourced from external research is flagged. I should be able to glance at a proposal and immediately see which claims lean on the literature versus which lean on repo evidence.</p>

