  <p><code>ee</code> was added when I realized I was repeatedly asking generic Claude to "evaluate this checkpoint and tell me if it's good," and the answers varied wildly. Sometimes Claude would invent a metric. Sometimes anchor on the wrong axis. Sometimes silently confuse training-loss with eval-R². Carving out a measurement-only lane with mandatory confidence tags + dated reports made eval calls auditable.</p>

  <p><code>ee</code> owns measurement and interpretation:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Checkpoint scoring.</li>
    <li>Sweep leaderboards.</li>
    <li>Spec-vs-measurement drift flags.</li>
    <li>Corpus audits.</li>
    <li>Plateau / collapse / noise classification.</li>
    <li>Early-stop recommendations (advisory only).</li>
  </ul>

  <p><code>ee</code> does not own training code (only <code>src/bot/run_state_config.py</code> threshold constants when a written BB-NNN approves), new metric proposals (that's <code>nn</code>'s lane), or phase-transition decisions (that's <code>bb</code>'s go/no-go).</p>

  <p>The signature feature is mandatory confidence tags. Every interpretive claim in every report carries <strong>HIGH / MED / LOW</strong>. Bare numerical readouts are HIGH-confidence-implicit. Speculative claims are LOW. No tag = report fails review.</p>

  <h4>confidence-tag policy</h4>
  <table class="conf-policy">
    <tr><td class="h">HIGH</td><td>bare numerical readouts; direct measurement</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="m">MED</td><td>interpretive claims with reasonable evidence</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="l">LOW</td><td>speculative claims; small-sample inferences</td></tr>
  </table>

  <p><code>ee</code> has two trigger modes:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fire-and-return</strong> (default) — invoked with a scoped task, runs the eval, writes the report, exits.</li>
    <li><strong>Resident</strong> (opt-in only on explicit user request) — stays alive polling <code>src/bot/run_state_checkpoints/</code> for new checkpoints during long-running sweeps.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Resident mode is explicit-opt-in only because of an early misfire: <code>ee</code> entered resident mode based on an inferred "looks like a long sweep" signal and sat polling for hours. The hard rule was carved the next day.</p>

  <p>The other rule that shapes <code>ee</code>'s operation: <strong>all cross-agent routing through <code>bb</code>.</strong> <code>ee</code> never directly pings <code>pp</code> / <code>nn</code> / <code>mm</code> / <code>ww</code>. Findings name an escalation target ("→ pp: eval script can't score a <code>--no-save</code> smoke") as a <em>recommendation</em> to <code>bb</code>, not a directive. The <code>--no-save</code> story is the one that established the rule: a BB-NNN plan declared "R² ∈ [0.15, 0.30]" as a gate, but <code>ee</code> discovered the eval pipeline ran in <code>--no-save</code> mode that suppressed the checkpoint <code>run_state_eval.py</code> would have scored. Drift-flagged → <code>bb</code> → <code>pp</code> added a <code>--save-eval-checkpoint</code> flag. The drift would have masked a real failure mode silently otherwise.</p>

