  <h4>signature · metric request contract</h4>
  <div class="req-pair">
    <div class="req good">
      <div class="tag">✓ allowed · WHAT</div>
      <p>"I need per-stage average HP-remaining-at-end-of-fight surfaced via the existing stats dict, called <code>stage_hp_remaining_avg</code>. Wire it through the existing telemetry path."</p>
    </div>
    <div class="req bad">
      <div class="tag">✗ forbidden · HOW</div>
      <p>"Add <code>stats['stage_hp_remaining_avg']&nbsp;=&nbsp;sum(...)/len(...)</code> at line 432 of <code>learner.py</code>."</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The metric-request pattern turned out to be the cleanest cross-agent contract in the whole crew. "WHAT" + producer-decides-HOW survives refactors; "WHERE" + producer-just-types-it-in breaks the moment line numbers move or <code>learner.py</code> gets reorganized. The producer knows its own code; the consumer doesn't need to.</p>

  <p>War stories that shaped <code>dd</code>:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The "30–60% healthy band" gaffe.</strong> <code>dd</code> hardcoded a <code>leave_shop</code> healthy band of 30–60% based on a generic prior. My actual play data showed 93.6% of shop visits result in a purchase — true healthy band is 5–15%. Per the <code>feedback_personal_runs_baseline</code> memory, all decision-surface baselines now derive from my 529 personal <code>.run</code> files in <code>sts2Stats/uploads/runs/</code>, never from theory. The rule generalizes: when a decision-surface baseline is needed, the data is in the repo, not in the literature.</li>
    <li><strong>The Worker Status duplicate-rows bug.</strong> Workers showed up twice on the dashboard — once by IP, once by a connection-identity hash. <code>dd</code>'s DD-028 added a dedupe step keyed on human-readable <code>worker_name</code>, with a "stale (1 conn)" sentinel row for unmapped hashes. Pure render-side fix. <code>dd</code> did not edit the producer code that was emitting both rows.</li>
    <li><strong>The flat-scalar Chart.js bug.</strong> <code>dd</code>'s <code>_shopUpdateChart</code> helper had a condition that treated flat scalar arrays <code>[0.034, 0.037, ...]</code> as multi-series and silently rendered nothing. Affected 4 Shop charts simultaneously. Fix: tighter type-check (<code>Array.isArray(datasets) &amp;&amp; Array.isArray(datasets[0])</code>).</li>
  </ul>

  <p><code>dd</code>'s reporting is hybrid. Substantive changes get dated <code>DD-NNN</code> reports at <code>drafts/dd/current/DD-NNN-&lt;slug&gt;.md</code>. Small inline tweaks get a single-line dated bullet in <code>drafts/dd/dd-changelog.md</code>. Both formats coexist; the reporting tier matches the change.</p>

  <h3 id="ss"><span class="ham">#</span>story-sage · explainer</h3>
  <div class="spec" data-lane="comms">
    <div class="spec-bar"><span class="file">.claude/agents/story-sage.md</span><span class="right"><span class="lane-tag">comms</span><span class="model sonnet">sonnet</span></span></div>
    <div class="spec-body"><table>
      <tr><th>nickname</th><td><code>ss</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th>lane</th><td>comms</td></tr>
      <tr><th>added</th><td>2026-04-23 · third wave</td></tr>
      <tr><th>owns</th><td><code>SS-NNN</code> markdown briefs · OneDrive PDFs · NotebookLM customization prompt</td></tr>
      <tr><th>cedes</th><td>everything except documentation</td></tr>
      <tr><th>triggers</th><td>manual</td></tr>
      <tr><th>signature</th><td>11-section briefs · 5K–20K words · coverage &gt; brevity</td></tr>
