  </div>
  <div class="rule-float" data-lane="comms">
    <div class="rf-bar"><span>hard rule · ss</span><span>language stack call-out</span></div>
    <div class="rf-body">every brief covers language, packages, versions, and <em>why those specific choices</em> — sourced from the actual repo, never hand-waved.</div>
  </div>
  <p><code>ss</code> came in for a different reason than the others. I was vibecoding a serious distributed-RL stack and realized I didn't deeply understand the libraries I was using — what Harmony actually does to a managed assembly, why GAE-λ matters, how PyTorch's <code>Categorical</code> distribution computes log-probs. I started using NotebookLM (Google's audio-overview tool) for daily commute listening, but the audio quality depended heavily on the input brief quality. <code>ss</code> was built specifically to produce briefs rich enough that NotebookLM's audio is genuinely useful — not surface-level "here's what PPO does" but "here's why PPO clips the importance ratio, what happens if the ratio is corrupt, and which line in <code>learner.py:124</code> enforces it."</p>

  <p><code>ss</code> produces four artifacts per invocation:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong><code>drafts/ss/current/SS-NNN-&lt;slug&gt;.md</code></strong> — the markdown brief (canonical).</li>
    <li><strong><code>OneDrive/sts2Player-briefs/SS-NNN-&lt;slug&gt;.pdf</code></strong> — PDF rendering via Node.js <code>md-to-pdf</code> (Puppeteer). OneDrive auto-syncs to all my devices, so I can upload to NotebookLM from anywhere.</li>
    <li><strong><code>OneDrive/sts2Player-briefs/notebooklm_prompt.txt</code></strong> — the always-current customization prompt, overwritten every invocation. Single path so I always know where the latest is.</li>
    <li><strong><code>drafts/ss/log/SS-NNN-&lt;slug&gt;.txt</code></strong> — per-invocation archive of the prompt. Never overwritten — historical record.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Plus the <code>drafts/ss/ss-briefs.md</code> ledger.</p>

  <p>Every brief covers eleven sections in order: Title, Framing, Glossary, Theory, Design Space, What This Project Chose, Code Walkthrough, Trade-offs, References, Takeaways, What We Skipped. Length targets are 5K–7K (short), 8K–12K (medium), 12K–20K (long). Coverage &gt; brevity. Every external claim sourced; every code claim grep-verifiable.</p>

  <h4>customization prompt (overwritten every invocation)</h4>
  <pre class="field-block"><div class="pre-tag"><span>OneDrive/sts2Player-briefs/notebooklm_prompt.txt  ·  current</span></div><code>{`You're producing an Audio Overview podcast for a developer who vibecoded a
distributed reinforcement-learning system to play Slay the Spire 2. The PDF
walks through the PPO Learner Loop. Cover the eleven sections in order, but
emphasize: (1) the importance-ratio clip and why it exists, (2) the GAE-λ
trade-off, (3) the value-head silent-corruption story (BB-009). Skip the
multi-host TCP framing — that's a separate brief. Tone: conversational,
analogy-rich. Listener has Python and some ML background but hasn't built
PPO from scratch.`}</code></pre>

  <p>Two rules came out of war stories:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The "uses Python" hand-wave.</strong> An early brief glossed the dependency stack with one line. I pushed back. Memory feedback added: every SS brief covers the language, packages, versions, and <em>why those specific choices</em> explicitly, sourced from the actual repo (<code>pyproject.toml</code> / <code>csproj</code> / <code>requirements.txt</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>The repeated-basics problem.</strong> Later briefs were re-explaining "what's a tensor" every time. Prior-pod-awareness rule: read the SS ledger first, skim covered topics, explain basics from scratch only for new ones.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><code>ss</code> has no peer dependencies. Pure documentation lane. Reads everywhere; writes only to <code>drafts/ss/</code> and the OneDrive briefs directory.</p>

  <h2 id="orchestrate"><span class="num">§ 3</span>How they orchestrate</h2>
  <svg class="under" viewBox="0 0 90 8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M 2 4 Q 18 1, 32 5 T 60 3 T 86 5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
  <p>Nine specialists. The shape they make together is <strong>loosely-coupled peers + one central planner</strong>. <code>bb</code> is the coordination point; the other eight are peers grouped by lane. The hand-off matrix below shows where the lanes touch.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Blueprint Beacon (<code>bb</code>)</strong> is the coordination point. Plans flow through <code>bb</code>. Cross-agent work routes through <code>bb</code>. The user-approval gate lives in <code>bb</code>. But <code>bb</code> has no authority over the other agents — it can only spawn them once I've approved a specific phase.</li>
    <li><strong>The other eight are peers.</strong> They can pull information from each other at decision points, they can route hand-offs through <code>bb</code>, but no agent commands another. Each owns its own lane and emits artifacts other agents consume.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>There is no canonical pipeline file. The crew coordinates ad-hoc based on the lanes their specs define and the BB-NNN plans <code>bb</code> threads through. The closest thing to a pipeline is the LONG-route critique cycle that <code>bb</code>'s spec encodes — and even that is a pattern, not a hardcoded flow.</p>

