  <p>The only <strong>scheduled</strong> agent is <code>ww</code>. The only <strong>resident-watcher</strong> mode is <code>ee</code>'s opt-in. The only <strong>reactive-on-event-signature</strong> is <code>ww</code>. Everything else is manual or reactive-to-<code>bb</code>.</p>

  <p>Hand-offs happen through the file system. Every agent writes to its own <code>drafts/&lt;nickname&gt;/</code> directory:</p>

  <div class="filesys">
    <div class="pre-tag">drafts/  ·  per-agent directories</div>
    <pre>{`drafts/bb/    — plans + ledger
drafts/pp/    — diff summaries + smoke scripts
drafts/mm/    — diff summaries + changelog + bat snapshots
drafts/ww/    — fleet log + reports + bat snapshots
drafts/dd/    — DD-NNN reports + changelog
drafts/ee/    — EE-NNN reports + ledger
drafts/nn/    — proposals + design notes + rolling log
drafts/gg/    — dated Q&A reports
drafts/ss/    — markdown briefs + log archive
drafts/codex/ — codex sweep outputs (via the standing codex review loop)`}</pre>
  </div>

  <p>Cross-agent routing through <code>bb</code> is the load-bearing rule. <code>ee</code>'s spec puts it most strongly: "All cross-agent routing through <code>bb</code>. Never directly ping <code>pp</code>, <code>nn</code>, <code>mm</code>, <code>ww</code>, <code>gg</code>." Other agents have softer versions of the same rule. <code>bb</code> is the coordination point.</p>

  <p>There's also an out-of-band surface for cross-device material: <code>OneDrive/sts2Player-briefs/</code> and <code>OneDrive/sts2Player-plans/</code>. <code>ss</code>-generated PDFs and <code>bb</code>'s <code>bb-final</code> plans land there. I pick them up on phone, iPad, laptop without depending on the WSL working tree being awake.</p>

  <p>And there's <code>ntfy.sh</code> for critical notifications. Hard-stop events fire <code>curl -s -d "&lt;msg&gt;" ntfy.sh/&lt;topic&gt;</code> to push to my phone. <code>ww</code> uses this for fleet hard-stops. The autonomous overnight cycle uses it for learning regressions. Short messages, fired only on hard-stop or fix-success, never on routine acks. Pager discipline: if the phone buzzes, something is actually wrong.</p>

  <h2 id="codex"><span class="num">§ 4</span>Codex as the always-on second pair of eyes</h2>
  <svg class="under" viewBox="0 0 90 8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M 2 4 Q 18 1, 32 5 T 60 3 T 86 5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
  <p>One pattern that sits outside the nine subagents but threads through every one of them: <strong>codex review on every code change.</strong> Codex is OpenAI's CLI tool, called from within the Claude conversation via <code>cmd.exe /c "codex exec ..."</code> with output tee'd to <code>/tmp/claude-codex.log</code> for live observability via a <code>claude-codex</code> tmux session. Codex reads the change cold, from outside the conversation, and emits findings — GO / HOLD / HIGH / MED / LOW.</p>

  <p>The standing rule (<code>feedback_codex_review_loop</code> in memory): every code change routes through codex. Codex must GO before the change is reported complete. If codex 401s in WSL (which happens occasionally when the auth session expires), manual review is substituted with explicit disclosure.</p>

  <pre class="field-block"><div class="pre-tag"><span>codex_review_loop · flow</span></div><code>{`code change ──→ codex exec ──→ {GO}     ──→ report complete
                       │
                       ├──→ {HOLD}   ──→ revise + re-run
                       │
                       └──→ {HIGH/MED/LOW}
                            │
                            └──→ fold finding ──→ re-run`}</code></pre>

  <p>The friction is real — extra round-trip per commit. The catch rate has paid for itself many times. Three real catches:</p>

  <h4>three real catches</h4>
  <div class="catch">
    <div class="id">BB-035 · picker → shop fanout</div>
    <p>The <code>picker_sidecar</code> → <code>shop_sidecar</code> fanout payload was missing <code>schema_version</code>. Codex flagged HIGH. The 1-line fix landed before the change was reported complete. (This is the same payload bug that the 5-iteration <code>mm</code> saga eventually traced to — codex caught it from the other end.)</p>
  </div>
  <div class="catch">
    <div class="id"><code>deploy.bat</code> paren-escaping audit</div>
    <p>After <code>ww</code> fixed 13 occurrences of <code>&gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1</code> to <code>&gt;nul 2&gt;&amp;1</code>, codex flagged a ladder of <code>if ... ( call ... )</code> blocks where the <code>call</code>'s output redirection inside the parens needed escaping. Caught a latent bug that would have surfaced on next deploy.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="catch">
    <div class="id">MM-069 · <code>ConfigureAwait(false)</code> deadlock-risk</div>
    <p>The raw <code>NetworkStream.WriteAsync</code> + <code>FlushAsync</code> change was on the game thread. Without <code>ConfigureAwait(false)</code>, the await would resume on the captured context — and the captured context was the synchronization context Godot was running on, which means resuming on it would have deadlocked the game on the next frame. Codex flagged HOLD before land. <code>mm</code> added <code>ConfigureAwait(false)</code> and re-ran codex. GO.</p>
  </div>

  <p>Codex finds things the in-context Claude doesn't. The reason is mechanical: codex isn't in the conversation, so it isn't anchored on the framing the conversation has accumulated. It reads the diff cold. The adversarial-second-reviewer property is the thing — the agent in the conversation has too much context; the agent outside has just enough.</p>

  <h2 id="seams"><span class="num">§ 5</span>The seams — where lanes leak</h2>
  <svg class="under" viewBox="0 0 90 8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M 2 4 Q 18 1, 32 5 T 60 3 T 86 5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>

  <p>Lane separation works most of the time. When it leaks, it leaks at predictable seams.</p>

  <div class="seam-h"><span class="lake">◇</span> seam · <span class="nicks">mm ↔ pp</span> schema changes <span class="lane-dots"><span class="ld code"></span><span class="ld code"></span></span></div>
  <p>The C# mod emits JSON; Python decodes it. Schema changes are paired work — both sides have to land in the same engagement or the protocol-version mismatch silently corrupts every observation envelope. The rule is: any <code>mm</code> edit that changes the JSON shape must explicitly flag <code>pp</code>'s paired work in the diff summary's <code>## Downstream</code> section. Empty bullet "none" stated explicitly. Never silent. The MM-067 saga is what made this rule. The shop_sidecar fanout payload was missing <code>schema_version</code> because nobody on either side caught that the inner-payload contract had drifted.</p>

  <div class="seam-h"><span class="lake">◇</span> seam · <span class="nicks">pp ↔ ww</span> script ↔ Python <span class="lane-dots"><span class="ld code"></span><span class="ld ops"></span></span></div>
  <p><code>ww</code> owns the bats; <code>pp</code> owns the Python they invoke. When a Python script's CLI changes, the bat that invokes it has to track the change. The seam is loose because nobody currently <em>owns</em> "make sure the bat keeps invoking the Python correctly" — that responsibility is shared, which means it's nobody's. When this leaks, the symptom is a deploy that runs but the workers do something subtly wrong. Currently caught by codex more often than by either agent's spec.</p>

  <div class="seam-h"><span class="lake">◇</span> seam · <span class="nicks">dd ↔ producer agents</span> metric semantics <span class="lane-dots"><span class="ld comms"></span><span class="ld code"></span><span class="ld ops"></span></span></div>
  <p><code>dd</code>'s metric-request pattern is clean for new metrics. It's less clean for <em>changing</em> an existing metric's semantics. If <code>pp</code> changes how <code>tel_rec['stage_hp_remaining_avg']</code> is computed (say, switching from arithmetic mean to median), the dashboard panel keeps rendering — but the chart is now lying, because the panel's title still says "average." The fix is to treat metric semantics as part of the schema and require <code>pp</code> to flag <code>dd</code> when semantics change. This rule isn't in the spec yet. It's a leaked seam I haven't tightened.</p>

  <div class="seam-h"><span class="lake">◇</span> seam · <span class="nicks">bb ↔ everyone</span> autonomous-mode carve-out <span class="lane-dots"><span class="ld planning"></span><span class="ld code"></span><span class="ld ops"></span><span class="ld advisory"></span><span class="ld comms"></span></span></div>
  <p><code>bb</code>'s "no work happens without user approval" rule sometimes collides with overnight autonomous work. The <code>feedback_phase3_autonomous_mode</code> memory carved out a specific case: when I delegate Phase 3 readiness work, <code>bb</code> only surfaces "need approval" (security/risk) and "ready for phase 3 review" milestones; per-step asks are suppressed; <code>bb</code> defaults auto-approved within the phase. This unblocked overnight work without violating the per-phase-approval rule (the blanket approval IS the per-phase greenlight, scoped to one phase). It's also the most fragile rule in the crew because it depends on me remembering to bound the autonomous phase tightly. Get that boundary wrong and nine agents work all night on the wrong thing.</p>

  <h2 id="patterns"><span class="num">§ 6</span>Patterns that turned out to matter more than I expected</h2>
  <svg class="under" viewBox="0 0 90 8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M 2 4 Q 18 1, 32 5 T 60 3 T 86 5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
  <p>A handful of things I didn't anticipate going in but now treat as load-bearing:</p>

  <div class="pattern-block"><span class="label">[lane.negative-space]</span><p><strong>Lane separation through hard rules — <em>especially the negative space</em>.</strong> Each agent's spec lists what it does NOT own, explicitly. <code>pp</code> cedes mod work to <code>mm</code>. <code>mm</code> cedes Python to <code>pp</code>. <code>gg</code> cedes patch strategy to <code>mm</code>. <code>nn</code> cedes implementation to <code>pp</code>. The negative space is the load-bearing concept — without it, agents drift into adjacent lanes and I lose the contract that "asking <code>gg</code> won't accidentally edit <code>pp</code>'s code."</p></div>

  <div class="pattern-block"><span class="label">[approval.per-phase]</span><p><strong>Per-phase user approval as the actual veto.</strong> Sign-off-in-advance-on-a-five-phase-plan locks in five phases of decisions before I can correct course. Per-phase approval gives me a real veto at every step. It's annoying — five gates per plan instead of one — and it's the reason the project hasn't drifted into a five-phase mistake.</p></div>

  <div class="pattern-block"><span class="label">[delegation.metric-request]</span><p><strong>Auto-delegation with metric requests.</strong> "I need WHAT" is a contract a peer agent can satisfy correctly. "Add this exact line at line 432" is a request that will be wrong as soon as <code>learner.py</code> gets refactored. The metric-request pattern (<code>dd</code>'s signature) generalizes: cross-agent contracts should describe outcomes, not implementations.</p></div>

  <div class="pattern-block"><span class="label">[discovery.drift-flag]</span><p><strong>Drift-flagging as a first-class output.</strong> When <code>gg</code> notices <code>encode_state</code> doesn't match the game's actual state shape, he writes a Drift Flag — file path, line range, specific mismatch. He doesn't fix it. He doesn't auto-spawn a fix. The flag sits in the report and the relevant agent picks it up via the orchestrator on the next engagement. This decouples discovery from action and prevents two agents from independently fixing the same thing in different ways.</p></div>

  <div class="pattern-block"><span class="label">[review.out-of-conversation]</span><p><strong>Codex outside the conversation.</strong> Already covered above. Worth restating: the value isn't that codex is smarter; it's that codex isn't in the conversation. The cold read finds things the in-conversation read can't.</p></div>

  <div class="pattern-block"><span class="label">[memory.feedback-files]</span><p><strong>Memory feedback files as durable context.</strong> <code>~/.claude/projects/&lt;repo-hash&gt;/memory/MEMORY.md</code> indexes about thirty <code>feedback_*.md</code> files. Each file is one rule I codified after a real incident. Examples: "kill_all_terminals.bat is nuclear by design" (don't try to surgically preserve running instances), "Fleet-degradation diagnostic ordering" (read <code>runs.jsonl</code> content patterns FIRST, watch a headed game SECOND, infra layer LAST), "Personal STS2 runs are the good-player baseline" (anchor decision-surface baselines to my 529 <code>.run</code> files, not theory). Every memory file I add tightens the crew's behavior across all future sessions. This is how a multi-agent crew gets <em>better</em> over time — not by upgrading the model but by accumulating the project's hard-won lessons in retrievable form.</p></div>

  <h2 id="transferable"><span class="num">§ 7</span>What's transferable</h2>
  <svg class="under" viewBox="0 0 90 8" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M 2 4 Q 18 1, 32 5 T 60 3 T 86 5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
  <p>For anyone building their own multi-agent setup in some other domain, the parts that aren't STS2-specific:</p>

  <div class="checklist">
    <div class="pre-tag">compose-your-own-crew · checklist</div>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Carve specialists by lane, not by capability.</strong> "Agent that does Python" is a lane; "agent that's good at coding" is a capability. The first composes; the second doesn't.</li>
      <li><strong>Make every spec list its negative space.</strong> What the agent does NOT own. Without it, lanes drift.</li>
      <li><strong>Centralize planning, decentralize execution.</strong> One planner agent, gated by user approval, that spawns specialists once a phase is approved. The planner doesn't implement; the specialists don't plan.</li>
      <li><strong>Cross-agent contracts should describe outcomes, not implementations.</strong> "I need X exposed as a metric called Y" composes; "edit line 432 of file Z" doesn't.</li>
      <li><strong>Add an out-of-conversation reviewer.</strong> Codex, GPT, a separate Claude — whatever — reading the diff cold. The cold read catches things the in-context read can't.</li>
      <li><strong>Drift-flag, don't auto-fix.</strong> Discovery and action decoupled means two agents can't independently fix the same thing differently.</li>
      <li><strong>Memory files for compounding.</strong> Every rule that started as a real incident gets written down once and referenced forever. The crew doesn't get smarter; it gets less prone to making the same mistakes.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p class="closer">The crew is nine specialists today. There are anticipated future additions — a test agent that would own <code>tests/</code> and CI wiring, a perf agent for profiling and benchmarks, a debug/triage agent for production-log triage and crash analysis, a docs agent for <code>docs/</code> and external write-ups. <code>pp</code>'s adaptive-scope rule says <code>pp</code>'s lane shrinks as new specialists arrive. That's the design: residual-shrinking by construction. The crew composes by accretion, and <code>pp</code> is the buffer that absorbs whatever isn't yet someone else's. None have been retired. The lanes that exist were earned by recurring failure modes, and the failure modes don't go away by retiring the agent that catches them.</p>

